WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:04.129
We found evidence for a
gravitational wave background

00:00:04.129 --> 00:00:06.423
for the very first time.

00:00:06.423 --> 00:00:08.341
But what is
a gravitational wave,

00:00:08.341 --> 00:00:11.344
and what on earth is
a gravitational wave background?

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:13.596
So, gravitational waves
are ripples

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:15.473
in the fabric of space time.

00:00:15.473 --> 00:00:17.559
And if there is a lot of these
gravitational wave signals,

00:00:18.601 --> 00:00:22.021 line:76%
they can add together
and give you a gravitational

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:23.148 line:78%
wave background.

00:00:23.481 --> 00:00:27.694
So, we believe that supermassive
black hole binary systems

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:30.780
are creating this gravitational
wave background.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:32.615
So, when galaxies merge,

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:35.702
their central supermassive
black holes should also merge

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:38.163
and when they merge,
they create these gravitational

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:39.205
wave signals.

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.541
These are so low frequency

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:44.043
that we can detect them
when they have periods

00:00:44.043 --> 00:00:47.505
of about 30 years or
1 nanohertz,

00:00:47.505 --> 00:00:50.717
and it takes them
about 25 million years to merge.

00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:52.469
That means that there's
a lot of time

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:54.721
for another galaxy merger
to happen,

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.806
and another one,
and another one,

00:00:56.806 --> 00:00:58.808
and then there are
so many signals

00:00:58.808 --> 00:01:01.895
that they create this
gravitational wave background.

00:01:01.895 --> 00:01:04.981
So, this is really important
for us to understand

00:01:04.981 --> 00:01:06.566
how the universe works.

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.652
Do galaxies merge as often
as we think they do?

00:01:09.652 --> 00:01:10.737
Looking to the future,

00:01:10.737 --> 00:01:13.782
we're hoping to make maps of the
gravitational wave background

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:17.827
and to also find individual
merging supermassive black holes

